% Test cases for the external infusion pumps policy
% (external_infusion_pumps.le): synthetic claims, one or two per criterion
% of LCD L33794 for the modelled indications (the insulin pump, epoprostenol
% and treprostinil, subcutaneous immune globulin) and per payment condition
% of dmepos.le, each stating only what its record shows. Findings are
% recorded as the treating practitioner's, the reviewer's or the Council's;
% a finding nobody made leaves the answer conditional. The queries: whether
% the claim is payable (the first query, so "stage" reads its checklist),
% whether it is reasonable and necessary, and what minimal change would
% make it payable.

the target language is: prolog.

the knowledge base external_infusion_pumps cases includes these resources:
    external_infusion_pumps.

the knowledge base external_infusion_pumps cases includes:

query pay is:
    which claim is payable.

query rn is:
    which claim is reasonable and necessary.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

query flip is:
    which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that
        claim 1 is payable.

% A complete first-month claim for an insulin pump (E0784): type 1
% diabetes (E10.9, group 1), criterion A (C-peptide at 80 percent of the
% lower limit of normal, fasting blood sugar 180 mg/dl), criterion C
% (education, four injections a day for a year with frequent
% self-adjustment, five self-tests a day, HbA1c 8.2 percent), the
% practitioner and team, the order, delivery at home and proof of delivery
% (E0784 needs neither a face-to-face encounter nor prior authorization).
scenario insulin_pump_complete is:
    claim 1 is for the insulin pump.
    the HCPCS code of the insulin pump is "E0784".
    the insulin pump is furnished to Ann.
    the residence of Ann is private residence.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    the rental month of claim 1 is 1.
    the diagnosis code of Ann is "E10.9".
    the connecting peptide level of Ann is 80 percent of the lower limit of normal.
    the fasting blood sugar of Ann at the connecting peptide test is 180 mg per dl.
    Ann has completed a comprehensive diabetes education program.
    Ann took 4 insulin injections per day for 12 months before the insulin pump.
    the finding frequent insulin dose self adjustment about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "her log shows correction doses and carbohydrate-ratio adjustments at most meals over the past year".
    Ann self tested glucose an average of 5 times per day during the two months before the insulin pump.
    the glycosylated hemoglobin of Ann on multiple injections is 8.2 percent.
    the insulin pump of Ann is ordered by a practitioner managing multiple beneficiaries on continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion with a knowledgeable team.
    the order for the insulin pump is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the insulin pump is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the insulin pump includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the insulin pump includes order date.
    the order for the insulin pump includes description of the item.
    the order for the insulin pump includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the insulin pump includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the insulin pump was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the insulin pump was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the insulin pump.
    pay expects answers ["claim 1 is payable"].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers [].

% The insulin pump criteria: Ben by criteria B and D (a positive
% autoantibody test; on a pump before Medicare, six tests a day) — claim 1,
% covered; Cy's C-peptide at 150 percent with normal renal function and no
% autoantibody — claim 2, not covered; Dee's renal insufficiency (creatinine
% clearance 40) allows a C-peptide at 180 percent — claim 3, covered; Eve
% took two injections a day — claim 4, not covered; Fay uses a continuous
% glucose monitor instead of fingersticks and has recurring hypoglycemia
% documented — claim 5, covered.
scenario insulin_criteria is:
    claim 1 is for the first pump.
    the HCPCS code of the first pump is "E0784".
    the first pump is furnished to Ben.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    the rental month of claim 1 is 1.
    the first pump was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the diagnosis code of Ben is "E10.9".
    the beta cell autoantibody test of Ben is positive.
    Ben was on an external insulin infusion pump before enrollment in Medicare.
    Ben self tested glucose an average of 6 times per day during the month before enrollment in Medicare.
    the insulin pump of Ben is ordered by a practitioner managing multiple beneficiaries on continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion with a knowledgeable team.
    claim 2 is for the second pump.
    the HCPCS code of the second pump is "E0784".
    the second pump is furnished to Cy.
    the date of service of claim 2 is 2026-03-01.
    the rental month of claim 2 is 1.
    the second pump was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the diagnosis code of Cy is "E11.65".
    the connecting peptide level of Cy is 150 percent of the lower limit of normal.
    the fasting blood sugar of Cy at the connecting peptide test is 190 mg per dl.
    the creatinine clearance of Cy is 85 ml per minute.
    Cy was on an external insulin infusion pump before enrollment in Medicare.
    Cy self tested glucose an average of 6 times per day during the month before enrollment in Medicare.
    the insulin pump of Cy is ordered by a practitioner managing multiple beneficiaries on continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion with a knowledgeable team.
    claim 3 is for the third pump.
    the HCPCS code of the third pump is "E0784".
    the third pump is furnished to Dee.
    the date of service of claim 3 is 2026-03-01.
    the rental month of claim 3 is 1.
    the third pump was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the diagnosis code of Dee is "E11.65".
    the creatinine clearance of Dee is 40 ml per minute.
    the connecting peptide level of Dee is 180 percent of the lower limit of normal.
    the fasting blood sugar of Dee at the connecting peptide test is 200 mg per dl.
    Dee was on an external insulin infusion pump before enrollment in Medicare.
    Dee self tested glucose an average of 5 times per day during the month before enrollment in Medicare.
    the insulin pump of Dee is ordered by a practitioner managing multiple beneficiaries on continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion with a knowledgeable team.
    claim 4 is for the fourth pump.
    the HCPCS code of the fourth pump is "E0784".
    the fourth pump is furnished to Eve.
    the date of service of claim 4 is 2026-03-01.
    the rental month of claim 4 is 1.
    the fourth pump was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the diagnosis code of Eve is "E10.9".
    the beta cell autoantibody test of Eve is positive.
    Eve has completed a comprehensive diabetes education program.
    Eve took 2 insulin injections per day for 12 months before the insulin pump.
    the finding frequent insulin dose self adjustment about Eve is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "adjusts her doses daily".
    Eve self tested glucose an average of 5 times per day during the two months before the insulin pump.
    the glycosylated hemoglobin of Eve on multiple injections is 9.0 percent.
    the insulin pump of Eve is ordered by a practitioner managing multiple beneficiaries on continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion with a knowledgeable team.
    claim 5 is for the fifth pump.
    the HCPCS code of the fifth pump is "E0784".
    the fifth pump is furnished to Fay.
    the date of service of claim 5 is 2026-03-01.
    the rental month of claim 5 is 1.
    the fifth pump was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the diagnosis code of Fay is "E10.9".
    the beta cell autoantibody test of Fay is positive.
    Fay has completed a comprehensive diabetes education program.
    Fay took 4 insulin injections per day for 8 months before the insulin pump.
    the finding frequent insulin dose self adjustment about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "she adjusts every dose to her sensor readings".
    the medical record of Fay documents continuous glucose monitor use.
    the glycosylated hemoglobin of Fay on multiple injections is 6.5 percent.
    the medical record of Fay documents history of recurring hypoglycemia.
    the insulin pump of Fay is ordered by a practitioner managing multiple beneficiaries on continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion with a knowledgeable team.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 3 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 5 is reasonable and necessary"].

% The Council finds Gus's multiple injection program was a fixed-dose
% regimen with no self-adjustment: criterion C fails (the Council-style
% unsupported finding), and with it the pump.
scenario no_self_adjustment is:
    claim 1 is for the insulin pump.
    the HCPCS code of the insulin pump is "E0784".
    the insulin pump is furnished to Gus.
    the residence of Gus is private residence.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    the rental month of claim 1 is 1.
    the insulin pump was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the diagnosis code of Gus is "E11.65".
    the connecting peptide level of Gus is 90 percent of the lower limit of normal.
    the fasting blood sugar of Gus at the connecting peptide test is 170 mg per dl.
    Gus has completed a comprehensive diabetes education program.
    Gus took 3 insulin injections per day for 7 months before the insulin pump.
    the finding frequent insulin dose self adjustment about Gus is unsupported, according to the Council, because "the diabetes clinic notes record the same fixed doses at every visit and no adjustment by the beneficiary".
    Gus self tested glucose an average of 4 times per day during the two months before the insulin pump.
    the glycosylated hemoglobin of Gus on multiple injections is 9.4 percent.
    the insulin pump of Gus is ordered by a practitioner managing multiple beneficiaries on continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion with a knowledgeable team.
    rn expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

